WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:19.529
Charles Ponzi.

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:20.529
Bernie Madoff.

00:00:20.529 --> 00:00:23.039
Billy “Fyre Festival” McFarland.

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:28.759
History is littered with the misdeeds of conmen
who pulled the wool over the public’s eyes

00:00:28.759 --> 00:00:29.999
and made a killing.

00:00:29.999 --> 00:00:34.350
Some, like Frank Abagnale - the model for
Leonardo Dicaprio’s character in Catch Me

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:38.550
if You Can - pulled off cons so big they’ve
become legendary.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:42.510
But not even Abagnale could compete with our
subject today.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:46.980
Gregor MacGregor was many things: soldier,
pirate, pathological liar.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.210
He was also the man who pulled off the biggest
con in history.

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:56.950
In 1821, MacGregor arrived in London with
tall tales of a new Latin American nation

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.280
where fortunes could be made.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.550
Using his deadly charm, MacGregor managed
to not only swindle banks, investors, and

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:08.950
ordinary people out of the 19th Century equivalent
of billions of pounds, he indulged in fabrications

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:13.350
on such a colossal scale that even Europe’s
governments were fooled.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:20.530
But how did this Scottish nobody create a
con so big it swallowed an entire continent?

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:28.770
Join us as we explore the life of Gregor MacGregor,
the most amoral adventurer to ever live.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:35.200
Birth of a Conman
When he came to tell the story of his birth

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.850
many years later, Gregor MacGregor would never
let two details remain the same.

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.299
He was born into high Scottish nobility.

00:01:42.299 --> 00:01:45.640
He was descended from the legendary outlaw
Rob Roy.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:50.970
His ancestors were adventurers who founded
Scotland’s doomed colony in the Darien Gap.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.649
The odd thing was, he needn’t have bothered.

00:01:53.649 --> 00:01:58.229
Gregor MacGregor’s background was interesting
enough even without his tall tales.

00:01:58.229 --> 00:02:03.210
Born on Christmas Eve, 1786, MacGregor came
from, well, the MacGregors, a Jacobite Clan

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:08.209
that had only just been relegalized after
100 years as outlaws.

00:02:08.209 --> 00:02:13.510
While his dad wasn’t the chief, as MacGregor
would claim, his family were still notable.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:17.050
His grandpa had been an officer in what would
become the Black Watch.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.870
His father was a ship captain.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.860
In short, young MacGregor had action in his
blood.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:28.580
And in 1803, when MacGregor was only 16, that
blood would drive him to seek adventure in

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:29.680
the British Army.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.459
As luck would have it, 1803 was the year the
Napoleonic Wars broke out, and MacGregor was

00:02:34.459 --> 00:02:39.060
hurriedly posted to the island of Guernsey
to defend against French invasion.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.340
But that invasion never came.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:47.290
Instead, MacGregor wound up living a deeply
unadventurous life of waiting around.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:50.230
Not that his time on Guernsey was all a waste.

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:53.900
It was while on assignment that MacGregor
met his first wife.

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:58.720
Maria Bowater was the sort of catch young
enlisted men used to dream about.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.160
Her father was an admiral, her family members
were MPs.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.520
Oh, and they were absolutely, stinking rich.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:11.129
In no time at all, MacGregor had charmed his
way into marrying her, much to the horror

00:03:11.129 --> 00:03:12.330
of her family.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:16.600
No sooner had they tied the knot then MacGregor
was using his wife’s money to buy himself

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:20.110
the rank of captain and get transfered down
to Gibraltar.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:25.270
But while the weather may have been nicer
on Spain’s southern tip, life was still

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:26.270
boring.

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:29.910
For the next four years, MacGregor did nothing
but stand around, waiting for a French fleet

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.200
that never came.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:36.500
Then, in 1809, just as MacGregor was planning
to give up and return to Scotland, excitement

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:37.519
finally arrived.

00:03:37.519 --> 00:03:42.730
The year before, Napoleon had invaded Spain,
engulfing the whole of Iberia in the Peninsular

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:43.730
War.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:47.239
Sensing a chance to give Napoleon a black
eye, the British decided to intervene.

00:03:47.239 --> 00:03:52.069
And that’s how Gregor MacGregor found himself
in Portugal fighting Napoleon’s forces.

00:03:52.069 --> 00:03:55.439
Yet even in the heat of combat, MacGregor
couldn’t settle.

00:03:55.439 --> 00:04:00.260
In October, he sold his rank of captain and
departed the British Army for the Portuguese

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:01.260
one.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:05.959
But by May, 1810, he’d also got bored of
the Portuguese and resigned again.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:11.000
Come summer, 1810, MacGregor was back in Scotland,
feeling as unfulfilled as ever.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.270
But he was no longer merely “Gregor MacGregor.”

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:17.750
On leaving the Portuguese Army, MacGregor
had started telling people he’d been knighted

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:20.720
for his service, and was now “Sir MacGregor”.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.250
It was a stupid lie.

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:27.300
A dumb, egotistical little lie that shouldn’t
have changed anything.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.610
Yet change things it did.

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:34.810
When people didn’t question it, MacGregor
realized just how easy lying about even huge

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:36.530
things really was.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:42.080
It was a revelation that would soon take the
young man to some very bizarre places.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:50.120
Life with the Liberator
In April, 1812, a ship pulled into port near

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.930
Caracas, Venezuela, and disgorged a slender
Scot in his mid-twenties.

00:04:53.930 --> 00:05:00.100
It had been two years since MacGregor had
left Portugal disillusioned, and much had

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:01.100
happened.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.289
In 1811, his wife had died, and his in-laws
had cut him off financially.

00:05:04.289 --> 00:05:08.080
So MacGregor had left Britain, in search of
something - anything - that would keep his

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:09.080
mind occupied.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:13.100
Luckily, 1811 wasn’t short of opportunities
for a would-be adventurer.

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:17.419
That year, Venezuela had declared independence
from Spain, kickstarting the South American

00:05:17.419 --> 00:05:18.919
Wars of Independence.

00:05:18.919 --> 00:05:21.190
So MacGregor had decided to get involved.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:25.950
No sooner had he landed than then-Republican
leader, Francisco de Miranda, had appointed

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:27.449
him a cavalry officer.

00:05:27.449 --> 00:05:33.289
But it’d be an even bigger name in Caracas
society that MacGregor would soon get involved

00:05:33.289 --> 00:05:39.669
with, thanks to his 1812 marriage to Josefa
Antonia Andrea Aristiguieta y Llovera.

00:05:39.669 --> 00:05:43.569
Josefa, you see, was cousin to none other
than Simon Bolivar.

00:05:43.569 --> 00:05:48.020
Not that his newfound connection would immediately
work out well for MacGregor.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:51.949
Just months after his marriage, MacGregor
was in flight, running for safety as the First

00:05:51.949 --> 00:05:54.710
Venezuelan Republic fell to Spanish forces.

00:05:54.710 --> 00:05:59.290
Francisco de Miranda was captured, while both
MacGregor and Bolivar only escaped - heading

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.410
in opposite directions - by the skin of their
teeth.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:06.740
And so began the period of Gregor MacGregor’s
life where he kept crossing paths with the

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:13.210
greatest figure in Latin American history,
only for it all to keep going horribly pear-shaped.

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:18.590
In 1813, for example, MacGregor volunteered
in the New Granada army, only to be assigned

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:24.160
to a remote border town and miss Bolivar’s
Admirable Campaign and founding of the Second

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.199
Venezuelan Republic.

00:06:26.199 --> 00:06:30.940
In 1814, the two both wound up in Cartagena
after the Second Republic fell, but Bolivar

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:35.210
was forced into exile while MacGregor got
trapped in the siege of the city.

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:41.050
Finally, MacGregor reconnected with Bolivar
in Haiti in early 1816 and joined his latest

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:42.050
campaign.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:45.020
It was a campaign that would nearly cost MacGregor
his life.

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:48.220
By now, most of Venezuela was back under Royalist
control.

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:52.660
Bolivar’s plan was to land men at strategic
points along the coast, then coordinate a

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:53.759
mass uprising.

00:06:53.759 --> 00:06:58.180
Put in charge of 600 troops, MacGregor was
quietly dropped at Ocumare.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:03.039
Unfortunately, the plan instantly fell apart,
Bolivar was forced into retreat, and MacGregor

00:07:03.039 --> 00:07:07.789
and his men were left standing on the shore,
their mouths dangling open as their ride disappeared.

00:07:07.789 --> 00:07:12.599
They were trapped in Royalist territory, over
300km from the nearest Republican stronghold.

00:07:12.599 --> 00:07:18.319
They had no boats, limited ammunition, even-more
limited provisions, and were surrounded by

00:07:18.319 --> 00:07:19.319
swampland.

00:07:19.319 --> 00:07:23.720
At that moment, it must’ve seemed to the
600 men like death was a certainty.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:27.419
They hadn’t counted on MacGregor’s talent
for self-preservation.

00:07:27.419 --> 00:07:31.539
In a remarkable feat of leadership, MacGregor
led his men on a march right through Royalist

00:07:31.539 --> 00:07:36.170
territory, executing innumerable brilliant
maneuvers to keep the enemy at bay.

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:39.169
He tricked the Spanish troops into walking
into swamps.

00:07:39.169 --> 00:07:43.000
He had his men charge blockades on foot and
punch right through.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:48.160
It was like watching LeBron James lead Elmer
Fudd around a basketball court, with the dimwitted

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:53.819
Royalists fumbling every play while MacGregor
ran circles around them, laughing.

00:07:53.819 --> 00:07:58.000
And yes, as far as we know, we are the only
YouTube channel delivering history via Space

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:59.750
Jam 2 analogies.

00:07:59.750 --> 00:08:01.099
You’re welcome.

00:08:01.099 --> 00:08:05.699
After weeks of running battles, MacGregor
finally led his men into the safety of a Republican

00:08:05.699 --> 00:08:06.699
town.

00:08:06.699 --> 00:08:10.770
When news leaked of his 300km trek, the Republican
camps exploded.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:15.520
MacGregor became a hero overnight, his name
celebrated across the continent.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:20.350
Suddenly, everyone wanted to serve under him,
the guy who’d married into Bolivar’s family,

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:22.289
and then out-Bolivared Bolivar.

00:08:22.289 --> 00:08:27.030
But, sadly, this isn’t a video about Gregor
MacGregor, revolutionary general.

00:08:27.030 --> 00:08:31.789
It’s a video about Gregor MacGregor, conman
extraordinaire.

00:08:31.789 --> 00:08:35.910
Those soldiers all clamouring to serve under
him didn’t know it yet, but MacGregor would

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:42.880
soon lead countless of their number into early
graves.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:46.240
Third Time’s the Charm...

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:50.540
In the wake of MacGregor’s daring escape
from Ocumare, Bolivar personally awarded him

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:56.560
the Order of Libertadores - hopefully while
shuffling awkwardly and mumbling “sorry

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:58.460
I abandoned you back there, bro.”

00:08:58.460 --> 00:08:59.460
But MacGregor was disappointed.

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:02.490
He hadn’t gone through hell just to get
a shiny new medal.

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:04.350
No, he wanted a promotion.

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:05.350
A raise.

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:07.410
Something to show the world he was a hero.

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:12.790
If Ocumare wasn’t enough for his cousin-in-law,
MacGregor would just have to go one bigger.

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:17.320
That fall, mercenaries up in Pittsburgh began
to whisper about a Scottish man doing the

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:21.560
rounds, hiring people for a private invasion
of Spanish Florida.

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:24.380
The Scot was a Portuguese knight, a Venezuelan
hero.

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:26.930
More to the point, he was very generous.

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:30.380
Anyone who joined him was being given titles
to huge estates in Florida.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:35.110
For the rough and tumble men of Pittsburgh’s
dive bars, it seemed like a no-brainer.

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:39.949
Which is how in June, 1817, MacGregor was
able to sail for Amelia Island, Florida at

00:09:39.949 --> 00:09:41.730
the head of 200 mercenaries.

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:44.510
There was just one minor catch.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:48.550
MacGregor didn’t actually own the land he’d
been dishing out to his private army.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:52.530
But, hey, while spoil a good invasion, right?

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:54.540
Not when everything was going so well.

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:56.040
And it really was.

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:59.670
When the only Spanish garrison on the island
saw all these armed Americans, they assumed

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:03.300
it was the head of a full-blown US force and
surrendered.

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:07.180
Now all MacGregor needed to do was press his
advantage, take a few more forts, and he’d

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:09.750
soon have another victory to rival Ocumare.

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:11.310
But that didn’t happen.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:15.610
Instead, MacGregor declared Amelia Island
the Republic of East Florida, and then simply

00:10:15.610 --> 00:10:18.650
settled in to enjoy getting drunk and sunning
himself.

00:10:18.650 --> 00:10:22.839
For MacGregor’s men, this must’ve been
a “what the-?”

00:10:22.839 --> 00:10:23.839
moment.

00:10:23.839 --> 00:10:28.310
Then MacGregor started paying them in Republic
of East Florida dollars he’d made himself

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:29.700
and it became a “Whaaa-?!”

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:33.790
Finally, one morning in September, the mercenaries
awoke to find MacGregor had taken the ship

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:37.052
and remaining money and simply sailed away,
leaving them to their fates.

00:10:37.052 --> 00:10:42.620
At that point, they probably couldn’t even
summon a squeak of surprise to show how they

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:43.620
felt.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:47.050
Following the Florida failure, MacGregor badly
fell out of favor with Bolivar.

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:48.660
So he tried again.

00:10:48.660 --> 00:10:54.230
In 1818, MacGregor sailed back to Britain,
hired 500 more men, and this time tried to

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:55.550
conquer Panama.

00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:59.150
Landing a force of 200 at Porto Bello, he
took the city…

00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:03.270
...and then went straight back to lounging
around in the sun, drinking rum and failing

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:07.000
to do anything that might stave off a potential
Spanish counterattack.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:13.060
This last bit proved important when the potential
counterattack became an actual counterattack

00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:17.790
and MacGregor was forced to flee, once again
leaving his mercenaries to their fates.

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:22.110
By now, the Scot was starting to look less
like the capable general of Ocumare, and more

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:26.400
like a shallow egotist who’d just happened
to get lucky.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:30.620
But his name still carried enough shine for
MacGregor’s surviving men to follow him

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:32.260
on one last invasion.

00:11:32.260 --> 00:11:37.630
In October, 1819, MacGregor landed outside
a Royalist holdout on Venezuela’s coast.

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:42.550
It was the exact same checklist as Amelia
Island and Porto Bello: MacGregor takes city?

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:43.550
Check.

00:11:43.550 --> 00:11:46.180
MacGregor gets drunk rather than organize
defenses?

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:47.199
Check.

00:11:47.199 --> 00:11:51.520
When things go south MacGregor sails away
and leaves his men to their fates?

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:52.670
Double check.

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:55.439
This time, those fates were particularly gruesome.

00:11:55.439 --> 00:11:59.520
After the Spanish retook the city, they lined
up all of MacGregor’s surviving mercenaries

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:00.709
and had them shot.

00:12:00.709 --> 00:12:05.110
In the aftermath of MacGregor’s third colossal
screw up, Simon Bolivar didn’t so much throw

00:12:05.110 --> 00:12:10.560
the book at him as crush him beneath an entire
library of disapproval.

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:12.770
Bolivar declared his cousin-in-law a traitor.

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:17.429
He issued a decree that any Venezuelan who
saw MacGregor should kill him on sight.

00:12:17.429 --> 00:12:21.449
But while this was the end of MacGregor’s
time as a revolutionary warrior, it wasn’t

00:12:21.449 --> 00:12:23.100
the end of the man himself.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:50.000
When he resurfaced, it was going to be with
a con that would go down in history.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:13:19.549
The Con

00:13:19.549 --> 00:13:32.809
is

00:13:32.809 --> 00:13:51.040
On

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:57.140
Sprawling along the Caribbean coasts of modern-day
Nicaragua and Honduras, the Mosquito Shore

00:13:57.140 --> 00:14:00.640
is exactly as unappetizing as its name suggests.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:07.190
It’s a hot, wet, desolate place where life
is cheap, disease is rife, and mosquitoes

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:08.850
omnipresent.

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:13.830
In April, 1820, Gregor MacGregor hopped off
a boat and vanished into the mangrove forests

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:16.939
of this tropical Hell, desperate to lose himself.

00:14:16.939 --> 00:14:19.650
By now, it wasn’t just Bolivar who wanted
him dead.

00:14:19.650 --> 00:14:22.630
The Spanish, the Americans, the British…
all, for varying reasons, had MacGregor’s

00:14:22.630 --> 00:14:24.280
name on a hit list.

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:29.140
In the movie of MacGregor’s life, this would
be the low-point, the part where all seems

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:30.140
lost.

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:33.910
But you know what always comes after the low
point in any movie script?

00:14:33.910 --> 00:14:36.050
The part where the hero triumphs.

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:40.250
While hiding on the Mosquito Shore, MacGregor
came into contact with one of the local tribal

00:14:40.250 --> 00:14:44.830
leaders, a guy who styled himself King George
Frederick Augustus.

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:49.020
Being a natural charmer, MacGregor got the
guy drunk and spent a few days partying with

00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:50.020
him.

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:53.420
By the time he emerged from the king’s hut,
weary and hungover, MacGregor was clutching

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:57.380
a title deed to 8 million acres of tribal
land.

00:14:57.380 --> 00:15:02.050
This piece of paper would become the basis
for his audacious next move.

00:15:02.050 --> 00:15:05.540
Back in MacGregor’s native Britain, the
nation was gearing up for the coronation of

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:06.780
George IV.

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:08.350
There were street parties.

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:09.549
Dignitaries from all over the world.

00:15:09.549 --> 00:15:14.939
In fact, there were so many dignitaries no-one
noticed when one arrived from a country that

00:15:14.939 --> 00:15:15.939
didn’t exist.

00:15:15.939 --> 00:15:20.080
His Serene Highness Gregor MacGregor the First,
Sovereign Prince of the State of Poyais and

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:26.330
its Dependencies, and Cacique of the Poyer
Nation arrived in London in summer, 1821.

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:30.660
Just like with MacGregor’s fake knighthood
in 1810, no-one questioned his absurd new

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:31.660
title.

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:34.959
To understand why, you have to remember when
this story is set.

00:15:34.959 --> 00:15:39.450
In 1821, a whole bunch of new nations had
just exploded into existence: Mexico, Gran

00:15:39.450 --> 00:15:41.780
Colombia, Argentina, Chile, Paraguay, Peru.

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:46.240
For Londoners, it wasn’t much of a stretch
to believe Poyais might be among them.

00:15:46.240 --> 00:15:51.780
Especially when the Poyais envoy was related
by marriage to Simon-freaking Bolivar.

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:56.040
But MacGregor did more than just trade on
his connections to fool people.

00:15:56.040 --> 00:16:00.120
Under the pseudonym Thomas Strangeways, he
published a guidebook to Poyais, detailing

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:03.439
the nation’s government, its geography,
its climate, its architecture.

00:16:03.439 --> 00:16:08.559
This was serious imagination, something Tolkein
would’ve been proud to write, and it was

00:16:08.559 --> 00:16:11.490
shot through with glimpses of a utopia.

00:16:11.490 --> 00:16:15.100
There was the soil, so fertile it could support
three harvests a year.

00:16:15.100 --> 00:16:18.530
There were the riverbeds, where lumps of gold
lay along the banks.

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:24.000
And there was the fact that, uniquely for
Central America, Poyais had no tropical diseases.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:27.440
Trust us, that last one will soon seem bitterly
ironic.

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:31.520
But why did MacGregor go to all this trouble
to invent a fake country?

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:35.000
For an answer to that, you need to look to
London’s Scottish communities.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:38.449
That same summer, the Poyais envoy began visiting
them.

00:16:38.449 --> 00:16:39.449
He was offering them an opportunity, he said.

00:16:39.449 --> 00:16:45.160
An opportunity not just to better their own
lives by moving to Poyais, but an opportunity

00:16:45.160 --> 00:16:48.079
to establish Scotland’s first viable colony.

00:16:48.079 --> 00:16:51.770
A chance to make their country into a global
player.

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:55.330
All they had to do, Gregor MacGregor told
his fellow Scots, was to buy one of these

00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:57.770
plots of Poyais land he was selling.

00:16:57.770 --> 00:17:00.860
Fertile land that would set them for life.

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:07.160
Faced with such an offer, who could refuse?

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:11.870
The Bubble
By the fall of 1822, interest in Poyais was

00:17:11.870 --> 00:17:13.130
running rampant.

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:17.302
The first boatload of Scottish settlers had
just departed for the country, and a general

00:17:17.302 --> 00:17:20.530
bubble in Latin American securities was expanding.

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:25.780
So when MacGregor asked a bank to underwrite
Poyais bonds, they simply nodded and handed

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:27.360
him £200,000.

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:31.840
Using that money, MacGregor opened Poyais
consulates across Britain, adding to the scheme’s

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:32.890
veneer of legitimacy.

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:38.040
For MacGregor, these were the salad days;
a phrase which means the heyday of something,

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:41.730
which is an odd choice when you consider how
boring salad is.

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:46.330
By winter, investment in Poyais bonds had
generated over a billion pounds in today’s

00:17:46.330 --> 00:17:47.330
money.

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:49.500
And MacGregor was still selling land to gullible
Scots!

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:52.790
The second boat of settlers departed in January,
1823.

00:17:52.790 --> 00:17:57.890
But there’s one problem with dispatching
boatloads of settlers to a non-existent country.

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:00.540
What do you do when they finally arrive?

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:02.250
Well, let’s find out.

00:18:02.250 --> 00:18:05.530
In November, 1822, the first ship reached
the Mosquito Shore.

00:18:05.530 --> 00:18:09.500
As the boat approached land, the Scottish
settlers all donned their fanciest outfits,

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:14.070
and fired the gun so a Poyais boat would come
out and guide them to the capital.

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:19.070
Instead, the gunshot echoed across the empty
waters and mangrove swamps, answered only

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:20.530
by deafening silence.

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:23.910
At first, the settlers assumed they must’ve
made a navigation error.

00:18:23.910 --> 00:18:28.350
So they all had their stuff unloaded onto
a deserted stretch of coast, and sent the

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:30.210
captain off to find Poyais.

00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:34.260
It’s worth noting that these were not young,
hardy types desperate for adventure.

00:18:34.260 --> 00:18:38.880
Most of those caught up in the Poyais scheme
were older folks looking for an easy retirement,

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:40.590
or families with young children.

00:18:40.590 --> 00:18:45.570
Surviving on a deserted stretch of tropical
shore was definitely not within their skillset.

00:18:45.570 --> 00:18:49.840
In March, 1823, the second ship joined the
stranded settlers.

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:53.130
Incredibly, everyone still assumed their navigation
was off.

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:56.850
Even when the captain of the first ship returned
and said “so, I’ve sailed all up and down

00:18:56.850 --> 00:19:01.600
this coast and there’s no Poyais,” they
told him, “err, obviously there is.

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:02.600
Duh.

00:19:02.600 --> 00:19:03.600
Try again.”

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:04.880
So off the captain went again.

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:09.750
And the now hundreds-strong crowd of settlers
settled down to wait again.

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:14.070
That same month, a hurricane battered the
Mosquito Shore, destroying supplies, and wrecking

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:15.410
the remaining ship.

00:19:15.410 --> 00:19:16.610
Then the rains came.

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:19.960
Thick, heavy, endless rains that left the
settlers soaked.

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:22.780
Finally, in the wake of the rains, came the
mosquitoes.

00:19:22.780 --> 00:19:26.780
If you’ve ever been to the tropics in rainy
season, you’ll have seen just how thick

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:31.240
the air can get with mosquitoes carrying yellow
fever and malaria.

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:36.550
On the deserted shore, the insects mobbed
the unprepared settlers, biting them, infecting

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:37.550
them.

00:19:37.550 --> 00:19:39.570
One by one, the abandoned Scots began to die.

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:41.710
Babies passed away in their mothers’ arms.

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:44.390
Elderly women died, wracked with malaria.

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:48.810
Of the 200 or so Poyais settlers who reached
the Mosquito Shore, it’s estimated over

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:51.650
two thirds were killed by tropical diseases.

00:19:51.650 --> 00:19:53.210
It was a massacre by neglect.

00:19:53.210 --> 00:19:56.490
A death sentence as sure as a firing squad.

00:19:56.490 --> 00:20:00.290
The only reason anyone survived is because
a ship from British Honduras just happened

00:20:00.290 --> 00:20:02.760
to pass and alert the colonial authorities.

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:08.480
For the rest of 1823, the Royal Navy was overwhelmed
evacuating the survivors and intercepting

00:20:08.480 --> 00:20:10.350
ships headed for Poyais.

00:20:10.350 --> 00:20:14.280
By the year’s end, they’d stopped five
more settler ships, each carrying hundreds

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:15.280
of people.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:18.400
Had they reached the Mosquito Shore, it’s
likely the death toll of the Poyais scheme

00:20:18.400 --> 00:20:20.400
would’ve topped a thousand.

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:23.740
Back in London, news of the disaster first
broke in October, 1823.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:28.380
Immediately, lots of very angry people began
looking for Poyais’ charming envoy.

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:29.890
But they were too late.

00:20:29.890 --> 00:20:34.230
MacGregor had fled England just before the
story arrived, taking as much of his ill-gotten

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:35.990
cash as he could carry.

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:39.860
It was a horrific denouement to the wildest
con ever pulled.

00:20:39.860 --> 00:20:41.890
Unbelievably, it still wasn’t over.

00:20:41.890 --> 00:20:46.590
Gregor MacGregor wouldn’t be done with the
Poyais scheme for another fifteen years.

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:55.970
No Bad Deed Goes Unrewarded
From 1823 to 1838, MacGregor pulled his Poyais

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:58.080
con again and again.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:01.090
Incredibly, he never faced any serious punishment
for it.

00:21:01.090 --> 00:21:05.920
Even after the harrowing tale of the survivors
got out, no-one in Europe could accept Poyais

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:07.200
didn’t exist.

00:21:07.200 --> 00:21:09.250
MacGregor had simply lied too big.

00:21:09.250 --> 00:21:13.730
In the same way our brains wouldn’t be able
to process the revelation that, say, Hawaii

00:21:13.730 --> 00:21:19.710
wasn’t real, people in the 1820s just couldn’t
comprehend the idea that Poyais was fictional.

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:24.070
When MacGregor settled in Paris, the French
government even made him an advisor on Latin

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:25.580
American affairs!

00:21:25.580 --> 00:21:29.630
This despite MacGregor restarting his fake
bonds scheme in France.

00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:34.690
Although the scheme itself was busted in 1825,
MacGregor successfully blamed his French partners,

00:21:34.690 --> 00:21:36.840
and again got away Scot free.

00:21:36.840 --> 00:21:40.940
He even returned to London and got more bonds
underwritten for £800,000.

00:21:40.940 --> 00:21:45.880
While no investors bit this time, it wasn’t
because of the tragedy on the Mosquito Shore.

00:21:45.880 --> 00:21:50.540
It was because the Latin American securities
bubble had just burst, and no-one wanted to

00:21:50.540 --> 00:21:51.540
risk another.

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:55.230
For the next few years, MacGregor hawked his
scheme up and down Britain.

00:21:55.230 --> 00:21:59.080
While he never again hit the big time, he
always had just enough money coming in to

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:02.040
keep the fiction going, to keep people believing
in Poyais.

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:05.690
Finally, in 1838, MacGregor’s wife Josefa
died.

00:22:05.690 --> 00:22:10.490
Suddenly alone in gloomy Britain, the conman
seems to have at last given up on his con.

00:22:10.490 --> 00:22:14.270
That October, MacGregor got on a boat and
sailed back to Caracas.

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:16.180
He never mentioned Poyais again.

00:22:16.180 --> 00:22:18.690
So… this is the moment, right?

00:22:18.690 --> 00:22:20.300
The moment when MacGregor finally gets his
comeuppance.

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:24.180
I mean, he’s headed back to Venezuela, the
very place Simon Bolivar left orders that

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:25.940
he be killed on sight.

00:22:25.940 --> 00:22:33.390
If you’re hoping for a moral end to this
story, best switch off now and construct one

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:34.390
in your own head.

00:22:34.390 --> 00:22:39.180
See, Bolivar had died all the way back in
1830, by which time he incredibly unpopular.

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:42.550
With Bolivar’s name now mud, everyone was
like “I mean, do we really need to kill

00:22:42.550 --> 00:22:43.890
this random Scottish dude?”

00:22:43.890 --> 00:22:45.010
So they let MacGregor land.

00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:47.360
They even let him request an audience with
the government.

00:22:47.360 --> 00:22:53.010
At which point, Lady Luck dropped an utterly
undeserved gold nugget right into MacGregor’s

00:22:53.010 --> 00:22:54.010
lap.

00:22:54.010 --> 00:22:57.140
Remember Ocumare, the one time that MacGregor
actually acted like a hero?

00:22:57.140 --> 00:22:58.940
Well, General Carlos Soublette did.

00:22:58.940 --> 00:23:03.770
He’d been under MacGregor’s command during
that mission, and seen the conman at his best.

00:23:03.770 --> 00:23:08.200
Since he’d never followed MacGregor to Florida,
or Porto Bello, or even heard of Poyais, he

00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:10.610
had no idea of MacGregor’s true character.

00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:12.870
And now Soublette was a member of Venezuela’s
government.

00:23:12.870 --> 00:23:18.350
When he saw MacGregor, he basically let out
a cry and threw his arms around him.

00:23:18.350 --> 00:23:23.861
That was how, in 1838, Venezuela came to reinstate
Gregor MacGregor’s rank in its army, hand

00:23:23.861 --> 00:23:27.850
over twenty five years’ back pay, and give
him a generous pension.

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:32.350
For the next seven years, MacGregor lived
in Caracas, wealthy, happy, and respected

00:23:32.350 --> 00:23:33.350
by all.

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:39.520
He finally died a peaceful death on December
4, 1845, without having ever repented of his

00:23:39.520 --> 00:23:40.520
cons.

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:42.500
Sometimes, life really is unfair.

00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:46.570
Today, it’s estimated that, between the
Poyais scheme and all those times he abandoned

00:23:46.570 --> 00:23:51.460
his men, Gregor MacGregor was responsible
for the deaths of 500 people.

00:23:51.460 --> 00:23:57.190
Yet even though he never got his comeuppance,
his story offers us all an excellent moral

00:23:57.190 --> 00:23:58.190
lessons.

00:23:58.190 --> 00:24:01.300
It’s easy to fall into the trap of assuming
that the world is fair.

00:24:01.300 --> 00:24:05.930
That maybe bad things sometimes happen, but
- in the end - everything will always balance

00:24:05.930 --> 00:24:06.930
out.

00:24:06.930 --> 00:24:09.820
The tale of Gregor MacGregor laughs in the
face of this.

00:24:09.820 --> 00:24:15.790
Here was a man who treated other people as
disposable trash, only there to fuel his own

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:16.790
fantasies.

00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:21.570
A man who sacrificed literally hundreds of
lives to the God of his ego.

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:25.450
And he never tasted justice for even a single
second.

00:24:25.450 --> 00:24:30.840
Unique among figures we’ve covered, MacGregor
offers us a bleak but necessary warning.

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:33.230
Sometimes, good doesn’t triumph.

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:39.090
Sometimes, the happy, wealthy, respected people
are the ones who have caused untold suffering.

00:24:39.090 --> 00:24:43.340
Gregor MacGregor may be long dead, but there
will always be others out there like him,

00:24:43.340 --> 00:24:45.260
charming and amoral.

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:48.870
If his life shows us anything, it’s that
we should always be on the lookout for these

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:49.870
people.

00:24:49.870 --> 00:25:06.690
Even when they seem to be the most respectable
types you could ever hope to meet.

